  "review": [
    {
      "section": "time.clock",
      "confidence": "low",
      "note": "No conventional past/to idiom known to me, so used '<hour> hoonal rik'in <minutes> minuut' (hoonal = hour/o'clock, rik'in = with, minuut = minute loan) and 'rik'in yijach' (with half) for :30. Minute numerals use the ALMG vigesimal forms (o'laju 15, junmay 20, oob' xkak'aal 25, o'laju xkak'aal 35, kaak'aal 40, oob' roxk'aal 45, lajeeb' roxk'aal 50, o'laju roxk'aal 55). Doubts: whether 'rik'in' is the connective speakers use; whether speakers would use these vigesimal numerals rather than Spanish ones; the existing noon/midnight ('chaq' chaab'il' / 'chaq' xkab'') look doubtful too (Q'eqchi' noon is usually 'wa'leb'') but I left them. Needs a native check."
    }
  ]
